; 24-done.kurt

; In a *file*, `assume`/`let`/`pick` blocks close automatically the moment
; the indentation dedents (that's what every earlier lesson relies on).  In
; the interactive shell there is no indentation to dedent from -- every line
; is typed at whatever level you're currently on -- so the shell instead has
; an explicit keyword, `done`, that closes the current block right away,
; triggering exactly the same rule (impl-intro / forall-intro / exists-elim)
; a dedent would have triggered in a file.  A transcript:
;
;     !!![1] bool A, B
;     !!![2] use A implies B
;     !!![3] show B
;     show B                                     ; 3 claim
;     !!![4] proof
;     proof
;     !!!    [5]     use A
;         use A                                   ; 5 without proof
;     !!!    [6]     B
;         B                                       ; 6 by 5, 2
;     !!!    [7]     done
;     qed                                          ; 7
;
; `done` requires `indent` mode (lesson 54) to be switched on -- that's what
; makes the shell track levels/indentation at all -- and, like `break`
; (25-break.kurt), it only works interactively: using it inside a `.kurt`
; file raises a `ParseError`.
;
; This file has no runnable `done` for that reason; the fact below just keeps
; it checkable on its own.

bool A
use A
A
